  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "A rectangle with a diagonal of length \\(x\\) is twice as long as it is wide. What is the area of the rectangle?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}x^2\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{5}x^2\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2\\)",
    "D": "\\(x^2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}x^2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The diagonal forms a right triangle with the rectangle’s length and width. Let the width be \\(w>0\\), so the length is \\(2w\\). By the Pythagorean theorem,\n\\[\nx^2=w^2+(2w)^2=5w^2.\n\\]\nThus \\(w^2=x^2/5\\). The area uses this squared width directly:\n\\[\nA=w(2w)=2w^2=\\boxed{\\frac25x^2}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
